## Project State Summary

**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development. It derives formal specs from GNU C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation pipeline.

**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds per-utility Go packages, `pkg/` holds shared libraries (testutils, sys, format), `magefiles/` drives orchestration. Differential testing via `pkg/testutils` compares Go binaries against GNU reference binaries.

**Current state:** 17 commands implemented at generation-merged tags (tee, head, seq, echo, tac, nl, fold, expand, unexpand, cut, paste, uniq, comm, md5sum, sha1sum, sha256sum, sha512sum plus earlier ts, cat, wc, sponge). All releases from 00.0 through 05.4 are marked `implemented`. Code status shows "not started" for all use cases in the current branch (code lives at v1 tags).

**Target release:** rel99.2 — deferred ts specification and differential test. One use case: rel99.2-uc001-ts (spec_complete, code not started).

## Priority Analysis

Release 99.2 has a single use case for the `ts` moreutils utility. The spec is complete. ts is a line-oriented filter that prepends timestamps to stdin. It was the first utility built in the project (originally rel01.0) and has existing code at generation tags, but rel99.2 represents the current spec-complete checkpoint needing fresh code generation.

Per P2, implementation and tests should be split. With a 1-task limit, I propose the implementation task. ts is a simple utility (~80-120 lines) well within the 300-500 line budget.

```yaml
- index: 0
  title: "cmd/ts implementation (prd004-ts R1-R4): stdin timestamp prefixing with format, elapsed, and incremental modes"
  dependency: -1
  description: |
    deliverable_type: code

    required_reading:
      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd004-ts.yaml (all requirements for ts utility)
      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel99.2-uc001-ts.yaml (end-to-end execution path)
      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling)
      - pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage)
      - cmd/tac/main.go (reference for line-oriented stdin filter pattern and flag parsing)
      - cmd/nl/main.go (reference for line-oriented stdin filter with formatting)

    files:
      - path: cmd/ts/main.go
        action: create
        note: ts utility implementation -- timestamp-prefixed line filter

    requirements:
      - id: R1
        text: |
          Read lines from stdin and write each line to stdout prefixed with a
          timestamp followed by a space. When no format argument is given, use
          the default strftime format "%b %d %H:%M:%S". When a format string is
          provided as a positional argument, use it as the strftime format for
          time.Format (translate strftime sequences to Go time layout equivalents).
          Implements prd004-ts core timestamp prefixing requirements.
      - id: R2
        text: |
          Implement the -s flag for elapsed-time mode. When -s is set, display
          the elapsed wall-clock time since the program started instead of the
          current wall-clock time. Format as HH:MM:SS by default, or use the
          user-supplied format string if provided. Implements prd004-ts elapsed
          time requirement.
      - id: R3
        text: |
          Implement the -i flag for incremental-time mode. When -i is set,
          display the time elapsed since the previous line was printed (or since
          program start for the first line). Format as HH:MM:SS by default, or
          use the user-supplied format string if provided. Implements prd004-ts
          incremental time requirement.
      - id: R4
        text: |
          Implement the -m flag for monotonic clock. When -m is combined with
          -s or -i, use a monotonic clock source (time.Now() monotonic reading)
          instead of wall-clock time for elapsed/incremental calculations.
          When -m is used without -s or -i, it has no effect. Implements
          prd004-ts monotonic clock requirement.

    design_decisions:
      - id: D1
        text: |
          Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main() per the
          project's shared SIGPIPE handling protocol. This ensures ts exits
          cleanly when piped to a downstream consumer that closes stdin early.
      - id: D2
        text: |
          Use Go's bufio.Scanner to read stdin line by line. For each line,
          compute the timestamp string, write "timestamp line\n" to stdout.
          Flush after each line to match GNU ts behavior (line-buffered output).
      - id: D3
        text: |
          Translate common strftime format sequences to Go time.Format layout
          strings. At minimum support: %Y (2006), %m (01), %d (02), %H (15),
          %M (04), %S (05), %b (Jan), %T (15:04:05), %F (2006-01-02),
          %s (Unix epoch seconds), %N or %.s (nanoseconds). Read the PRD for
          the complete list of required sequences. For elapsed/incremental modes
          with default format, output HH:MM:SS as duration formatting.
      - id: D4
        text: |
          Parse flags using the standard library flag package. Accept -s, -i,
          and -m as boolean flags. The remaining non-flag argument, if any, is
          the format string. If both -s and -i are given, the last one specified
          wins (match GNU ts behavior, or follow the PRD if it specifies
          otherwise).

    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: AC1
        text: cmd/ts/main.go compiles without errors via "go build ./cmd/ts/"
      - id: AC2
        text: |
          Running "echo hello | go run ./cmd/ts/" produces a line matching the
          pattern "<timestamp> hello" where timestamp matches the default
          "%b %d %H:%M:%S" format (e.g., "Mar 06 14:30:05 hello")
      - id: AC3
        text: |
          Running with -s flag produces elapsed time from program start in
          HH:MM:SS format prefixed to each line
      - id: AC4
        text: |
          Running with -i flag produces incremental time since previous line
          in HH:MM:SS format prefixed to each line
      - id: AC5
        text: |
          Running with a custom format argument (e.g., "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
          produces timestamps in the specified format
      - id: AC6
        text: |
          The binary exits 0 on normal completion and handles SIGPIPE gracefully
          (exits 0 when piped to head or similar)
      - id: AC7
        text: No lint warnings from "golangci-lint run ./cmd/ts/"
```